WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.981
Choosing a model for Apple Silicon starts with the workload.

00:00:03.981 --> 00:00:12.540
A casual assistant, coding helper, summarizer, document reviewer, and extraction workflow may each need different model behavior.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.317
Model size and parameter count matter, but they are not the whole story.

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:26.142
A smaller instruction-tuned model with the right prompt format can be more useful than a larger model that is slow or poorly matched.

00:00:26.142 --> 00:00:30.255
Quantization level is a practical memory and quality decision.

00:00:30.255 --> 00:00:41.933
More aggressive quantization can reduce RAM requirements and make a model fit on a lower-memory Mac, but it may reduce reasoning quality, instruction following, or consistency.

00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:48.103
Less aggressive quantization may improve quality but require more memory and run more slowly.

00:00:48.103 --> 00:00:52.615
Testing realistic prompts is better than choosing by filename alone.

00:00:52.615 --> 00:00:55.733
Context length should be selected for the task.

00:00:55.733 --> 00:01:03.762
Long-document workflows may need more context, but larger context windows increase memory use and prompt processing time.

00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:09.534
If the workflow is short chat, a very large context setting may simply waste resources.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:19.818
Apple Silicon can be strong for local workflows, but model size, context length, runtime overhead, and other applications still compete for unified memory.

00:01:19.818 --> 00:01:21.941
Licensing and provenance matter.

00:01:21.941 --> 00:01:30.434
Read model cards where available, track the source of model files, and understand whether the license permits your intended use.

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:35.477
Local execution does not erase license obligations or supply chain concerns.

00:01:35.477 --> 00:01:43.704
A good model choice aligns memory, speed, quality, licensing, trust, and privacy needs with the hardware actually available.
